The implementation and test are coherent:
- Fixture files exist at repo root `.fah/skills/{create-goal,fa-self-config}/SKILL.md` and their sha256 match the pinned fingerprints exactly.
- The new test covers the filed device state (already-ghosted js-apps empty dir), prune-on-remove, user-file survival, and end-to-end builtin read.

Now, per the TDD/review guidelines I should do a final sanity check of the remaining review dimensions, then decide:

1. Is there any code finding? Let me think hard about `_retireSeededCopy` semantics:

```dart
final body = (await env.readTextFile('$dir/SKILL.md')).valueOrNull;
if (body != null) {
  if (!isStale(...)) return;
  await env.remove('$dir/SKILL.md');
}
final leftovers = await env.listDir(dir);
if (leftovers.isOk && leftovers.valueOrNull!.isEmpty) {
  await env.remove(dir);
}
```

- When SKILL.md exists but is user-customized → return early, dir untouched. ✓
- When SKILL.md stale → remove file; if dir now empty → remove dir. ✓
- When SKILL.md already gone (ghost dir) → body null → falls through → listDir → empty → remove dir. ✓ (repairs filed device state)
- When dir has other files (user file) → leftovers non-empty → stays. ✓
- When dir missing entirely → readTextFile errors/null → listDir errors → no remove, no throw. ✓
- `leftovers.valueOrNull!` — guarded by isOk, safe.

Edge: `listDir` returns entries — does it include directories themselves (e.g. '.'/'..')? On MemoryExecutionEnv the test proves it lists children only. On real device envs (IO/POSIX), listDir is the same core implementation used everywhere for skill discovery — consistent behavior. If a real env listed '.', the dir would never be pruned — a missed optimization, not a breakage; and discovery code uses the same listDir, so semantics are consistent. Fine.

Edge: race with a user writing a file between listDir and remove → remove(dir) on a non-empty dir → on POSIX would fail (dir not empty) → throws → caught by `on Object` → best-effort. Fine.

Edge: remove(dir) failure on non-prunable dirs (permissions) → caught. Fine.

2. CRAP ratchet: CC(_retireSeededCopy) — count decision points: try(1?) + if body!=null(1) + if !isStale(1) + if(1) + &&(1) + catch(1?) ≈ 6-7. The new test exercises nearly all branches → high coverage via the merged shard lcov → CRAP ≈ 6-14 < 30 threshold. The old code had CC ~12-14 and passed. Improvement, not regression.

